verdict=IN-SCOPE: the approved plan's section 1 names these three fixes line by line; addSky joined the same loop as four more controls of the identical class, named in the finding. Self-judged.
# Self-judged. Docs-free, three files, and the approved plan's section 1 names it
# almost line by line:
# 
#   "Four unreached — #mColorPick, #mPaint, #mErase, #mClearBrush... One #addColor
#    and one #addBrush in the maskPlaceOpen branch of tools/control-walk.mjs
#    reaches them. Fix, do not excuse."
#   "Two label-versus-tooltip — #mOutline reads 'Show mask' while its tooltip says
#    'hide overlay photo'; #mMatte the same shape... Make the label carry it."
#   "Two glyph-named mask-row buttons... Give them accessible names."
# 
# All three done. Measured: the sweep reads 55 controls to answer for, against 72
# on main, with zero mask controls unreached.
# 
# ONE THING WENT BEYOND THE PLAN'S WORDS AND IS NAMED RATHER THAN ABSORBED.
# The plan listed four unreached controls because that is all that was visible in
# the sample it was written from. The run showed four more of the identical class
# — #mSkyByColour, #mSkyFixAdd, #mSkyFixCut, #mSkyFixClear, which exist only while
# a Sky mask is selected — so "addSky" joined the same loop, in the same lines the
# plan authorised changing. It is the same fix for the same class rather than new
# scope, and leaving them would have been the "excused by unreachability rather
# than measured" state the plan's own sentence rejects. It costs the walk real
# time, because a sky is DETECTED, so the branch waits on the detection landing
# rather than on a clock.
# 
# Not claimed: the full walk suite is still running. This commit is to the work
# branch only. The staging push waits for that result, which is what the plan's
# verification gates on.
